──────────────────────────────
Smart Scale: “Applause-Per-Gram”
──────────────────────────────
Goal  
Tweet 👏 emojis every time the measured mass drops by **one new decimal** on the low side (i.e., 0.1 g,ences).

Example walk-through  
• First reading on the scale: 75.0 kg  
• Later the scale reads: 74.9 kg → **first decimal** has changed ⇒ tweet 👏  
• Then: 74.8 kg → **second** decimal change ⇒ tweet 👏👏  
• Then: 74.7 kg ⇒ tweet 👏👏👏 … and so on.

────────────────
Hardware
────────────────
1. Load-cell platform (kitchen or bathroom scale, 5 kg or 150 kg range).  
2. HX711 24-bit ADC (sparkfun/aliexpress).  
3. ESP32-S3 (built-in Wi-Fi, >240 MHz, Arduino-compatible).  
4. 3.3 V power (USB-C cable or Li-Po + charger).  
Optional: OLED for local read-out.

────────────────
Wiring
────────────────
HX711 → ESP32  
VCC → 3 V3  
GND → GND  
DT  → GPIO 17  
SCK → GPIO 16  

Load cell:  
Red → → E+  
Black → E-  
White → A-  
Green → A+  

────────────────
Arduino Sketch
────────────────
Install libraries in the Arduino IDE:  
• “HX711” by Bogdan Necula  
• “WiFi.h” & “TwitterAPI.h” by witnessmenow (handles OAuth 1.0a)

Create Twitter (X) developer app → copy Bearer token, API Key/Secret, Access Token/Secret.

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h>
#include <HX711.h>
#include <TwitterAPI.h>

// ----------- Wi-Fi -----------
const char* ssid     = "YOUR_SSID";
const char* password = "YOUR_PASSWORD";

// ----------- Twitter -----------
const char* apiKey       = "YOUR_API_KEY";
const char* apiSecret    = "YOUR_API_SECRET";
const char* token        = "YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN";
const char* tokenSecret  = "YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET";

// ----------- pins -----------
const int LOADCELL_DOUT_PIN = 17;
const int LOADCELL_SCK_PIN  = 16;

HX711 scale;
TwitterAPI twitter(apiKey, apiSecret, token, tokenSecret);

// ----------- config -----------
const float calibration_factor = 1060.0f; // adjust with known weight
const char* tweetPrefix = "🏆 Scale says: ";

// state
float lastStableKg = 0.0f;
int lastTenths = 0;       // 0-9 of the 0.1 kg digit
unsigned long lastTweetMs = 0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.begin(ssid, password);
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) delay(500);
  Serial.println("WiFi OK");

  scale.begin(LOADCELL_DOUT_PIN, LOADCELL_SCK_PIN);
  scale.set_scale(calibration_factor);
  scale.tare();               // power-on zero

  lastStableKg = roundf(scale.get_units() * 10) / 10.0f;
  lastTenths = int(lastStableKg * 10) % 10;
}

void loop() {
  if (scale.wait_ready_timeout(200)) {
    float kg = scale.get_units();
    float rounded = roundf(kg * 10) / 10.0f;   // 0.1 kg resolution
    int tenths = int(rounded * 10) % 10;

    // Detect downward change in the 0.1 kg digit
    if (tenths != lastTenths && rounded < lastStableKg) {
      int claps = (int)((lastStableKg - rounded) * 10 + 0.5f);  // # of 0.1 kg drops
      String tweet = tweetPrefix;
      for (int i = 0; i < claps; i++) tweet += "👏";
      tweet += "  (" + String(rounded, 1) + " kg)";
      Serial.println(tweet);

      if (twitter.sendTweet(tweet)) {
        lastStableKg = rounded;
        lastTenths = tenths;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

────────────────
Calibration
────────────────
1. Place a known weight (e.g., 1 kg).  
2. Adjust `calibration_factor` until `scale.get_units()` returns the correct mass.  
3. Tare again when empty.

